		<div class="long">
			<div class="head">
									<div class="options">
						<a href="javascript:;" onclick="togglePostFold(event,1582261)" class="btn btn-default btn-small btn-sm">Fold</a>
					</div>
								<div class="title" id="post-title-1582261">
																		
															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/soullesssingularity" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(637830); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=637830&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1609233409" alt="SoullessSingularity" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=637830)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/soullesssingularity" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(637830); return false;" >SoullessSingularity</a></span> <span class="odate time_1349556154 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">06 Oct 2012 20:42</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-1582261">
									

<p>I'm not a great proser so do take my critiques with a grain of salt (and possibly anything else I do in the future).</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">Bunch&nbsp;of&nbsp;Nitpicking&nbsp;Click&nbsp;to&nbsp;Show</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">Hide&nbsp;the&nbsp;Nitpicking</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>Mr. Brashin stood resolutely in one of the greeting rooms the club used to meet with those not “in the know.”</p>
</blockquote>
<p>For the fact that, uh, everything else uses gentlemanly words like 'wont' and 'contempt' and 'professionalism', the phrase &quot;in the know&quot; seems incredibly out of place. You would think that the gentlemen would have a better way of saying it.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Irritated, he asked himself aloud: “Eight minutes late already, where has the punctuality of this new generation gone?&quot;</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Wait, I thought he himself was late in the first paragraph? Why did you take so long to clear this out, as opposed to a 2 paragraph rant about lateness and gentlemanliness and other shit? Hell, the whole thing could've been condensed into 'Mr. Brashin stood resolutely in one of the greeting rooms the club used to meet with those not “in the know.”', since its subsequent sentences already establish his old-timey gentlemanly whathaveyou dickery.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>They had no sense of real import beyond themselves, and he had yet to meet a young person with the proper sense of, he searched for the proper word, gravitas. Yes, that was it.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I could imagine the 'he searched for the proper word' would be better in dashes, since it seems fairly, uh, interrupting thought.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Irritated, he asked himself aloud:</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I don't know why colon.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The hands spun crazily for a handful of moments</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Haha, I see what you did there.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<blockquote>
<p>Swallowing nervously, he replaced the silver watch in his pocket and straightened his tie. He turned on his heel to face the door, his heart pounding. The footsteps were growing louder, and beads of sweat broke out on his forehead. He wiped them away quickly with the back of his hand. Dignity was all well and good, but this waiting was intolerable. The click of shoe on hardwood stopped just outside the stately meeting room door, and the handle turned slowly.</p>
<p>Unexpectedly, Mr. Brashin felt his eyes sting and his vision blurred for a moment with tears. He fought them back sternly. If he had to go, he would not go crying. Finally the door opened, and there was Mr. Marshall, smiling at him sadly. A rather large man stood just behind and to the right of him, but the brute was unimportant, in the larger scheme of things. Eric Brashin only had eyes for his employer.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I find almost all of this largely unnecessary. Largely. Yeah okay, he's going to be shot and he's trying to be like a gentleman about it. You can likely condense that and not lose any meaning in it, like so:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Unexpectedly, Mr. Brashin felt his eyes sting and his vision blurred for a moment with tears. He fought them back sternly. If he had to go, he would not go crying. The click of shoe on hardwood stopped just outside the stately meeting room door, and the handle turned slowly. The door opened, and there was Mr. Marshall, smiling at him sadly. A rather large man stood just behind and to the right of him, but the brute was unimportant, in the larger scheme of things. Eric Brashin only had eyes for his employer.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Now normally I would ask you to cut out some adjectives and adverbs but I assume you're trying to go for the more oldtimey speak so I won't actually jump on you. Except the use of 'sternly', which seems odd to me but that may be just me.</p>
<p>Otherwise, I don't really have specific portions to pick out, but it kind of pisses me off that it took half the story to actually get to the story. The rest was relatively unimportant bs that could've been condensed into a paragraph or two in order to put up a setting and give us a tone. I do appreciate the whole 'employee for 20 years' and 'loose ends' bit, since I don't actually pay any attention to tales beyond upvoting/downvoting most of the time. Details like that would probably just have gone over my head if you had chosen not to include it. So, meh, since it didn't actually move me in any way. It just told a narration, nothing more and nothing less.</p>

							</div>

										<div class="signature">
					<hr class="signature-separator"/>
					

<p>Living the dream, or dreaming the life?</p>

				</div>
					
					
